                // If pre.tail <= cur, we're done
                // Otherwise, cur_idx > cur, which means cur >= cur_idx + 1 is false, 
                // so cur > cur_idx + 1 is also false. But we need to be careful here.
                // Actually, if cur_idx > cur, then cur_idx + 1 > cur, so !(cur > cur_idx + 1).
                // But we want to show (cur > cur_idx + 1) || (pre.tail <= cur).
                // If pre.tail <= cur, we're done.
                // If pre.tail > cur, then we need cur > cur_idx + 1.
                // But we know cur_idx > cur, so cur_idx + 1 > cur + 1 > cur, so cur > cur_idx + 1 is false.
                // This seems problematic. Let's re-examine the definition.
                // 
                // Looking at no_overlap_with definition:
                // For CombinerState::Appending { cur_idx, tail } and CombinerState::Reading(ReaderState::Guard { start, end, cur, val }):
